<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gyeongju</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seorabeol, Geumseong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gyeongju is a city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> that was the capital of Silla for nearly a thousand years, until 935. It is rich in royal tombs, temples and palace sites, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulguksa">Bulguksa</a> temple, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seokguram">Seokguram</a> grotto and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheomseongdae">Cheomseongdae</a> observatory, and its historic areas are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. It is often called a museum without walls.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gyeongju.jpg" alt="Gyeongju" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kyoww (montage) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gyeongju_montage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gyeongju lies in the far south-eastern corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-gyeongsang-province">North Gyeongsang Province</a>, 370 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> and 55 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daegu">Daegu</a>. It covers 1,324 square kilometres, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> lies to its east, while low mountains, outliers of the Taebaek range, are scattered around it. Ulsan borders it on the south and Pohang on the north. Between 1895 and 1955 the area was called Gyeongju County. The city and the county were united in 1995, and it is now an urban and rural complex.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Gyeongju first enters non-Korean records as Saro-guk, in the Samhan period. Korean records, probably based on the dynastic chronicles of Silla, say that Saro-guk was founded in 57 BCE, when six small villages in the area united under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bak-hyeokgeose">Bak Hyeokgeose</a>. As the kingdom grew it took the name Silla. In the Silla period the city was called Sŏrabŏl, a name that was to evolve into a Korean word for a capital city, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, and also Gyerim, meaning rooster's forest, and Geumseong, meaning city of gold or metal, that is, an impenetrable fortress.</p><p>At its height, between the 7th and 9th centuries, Silla ruled about two-thirds of the Korean peninsula. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a>, Sŏrabŏl had more than 170,000 households, or between 800,000 and 900,000 inhabitants, and the city is estimated to have had a million residents, with later Silla ranking Gyeongju as the fourth largest city in the world. The Hwarang, a military cadet corps of young aristocrats trained in the academic and martial arts, are associated with the city, and generals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-yu-sin">Kim Yu-sin</a> played a central part in Silla's unification of the peninsula. Gyeongju's notable figures included the scholars Seol Chong and Ch'oe Ch'i-wŏn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gyeongju is the main destination in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> for visitors interested in the heritage of Silla and the architecture of Joseon, and is often called the museum without walls. Its tumuli, Buddhist sites, fortresses and palace sites include the Royal Tomb Complex, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheomseongdae">Cheomseongdae</a> observatory, one of the oldest surviving astronomical observatories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, and the Anapji royal pond garden. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seokguram">Seokguram</a> grotto, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulguksa">Bulguksa</a> temple, the Gyeongju Historic Areas and Yangdong Folk Village are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>. The city has 31 National Treasures, and its national museum houses 16,333 artefacts. Namsan, the sacred mountain, is covered with Buddhist shrines and statues, and Gyeongju is the birthplace of Cheondoism.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gyeongju” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…politics of the Three Kingdoms: Silla was for a time a near-protectorate, as the stele and a bronze vessel inscribed with Gwanggaeto's name found in a Silla tomb in <b>Gyeongju</b> attest, while Baekje's long rivalry with Goguryeo drove its alliances with the southern Chinese courts and Yamato Japan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>King Munmu, who completed Silla's unification, is said to have asked for burial at sea so that he could become a dragon guarding Korea; a rock islet off the coast near <b>Gyeongju</b> is held to be his tomb.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 683 Anseung was summoned to <b>Gyeongju</b>, given the Silla royal surname Kim and settled there.</blockquote><a href="/state/bodeok">Bodeok</a></li><li><blockquote>Jinhan formed in the 1st century BCE in the <b>Gyeongju</b> and Daegu area east of the Nakdong river.</blockquote><a href="/state/jinhan">Jinhan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 927 Gyeon Hwon sacked the Silla capital at <b>Gyeongju</b> and forced King Gyeongae to kill himself, then defeated Wang Geon of Goryeo near Daegu.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-baekje">Later Baekje</a></li></ul></section>
